{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And as for me, when I came from Paddan, etc.</b> Even though I am imposing upon you to transport me for burial in the land of Canaan — that is not what I did for your mother, for she died near Beit Lechem." "<b>A stretch of land.</b> A land measure — and it is two thousand cubits, like the measure of the Shabbat boundary, according to the words of Rabbi Moshe HaDarshan. And do not say that rains prevented me from transporting her to bury her in Chevron — it was the dry season, when the ground is hollow and perforated like a sieve." "<b>There I buried her.</b> And I did not transport her even to Beit Lechem to bring her into the land, and I know that there is resentment in your heart against me. But know that by divine utterance I buried her there — so that she would be a help to her children when Nevuzaradan exiles them, and they would be passing by there: Rachel emerges from her grave and weeps and seeks mercy for them, as it is said, ״קוֹל בְּרָמָה נִשְׁמָע״ and so on (Jeremiah 31), and the Holy One, blessed be He, answers her: ״יֵשׁ שָׂכָר לִפְעֻלָּתֵךְ נְאֻם ה' וְשָׁבוּ בָנִים לִגְבוּלָם״ (Jeremiah 31). And Onkelos translated כְּרוּב אַרְעָא — the measure of a day's plowing. And I say that they had a fixed measure they called 'one plow-furrow,' קורדיי״א בְּלַעַז, as we say כָּרֵיב וְתָנֵי — just as one measures a canal by the כַּרְבָּא."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:03:59.996183489Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}